nding up in coding agent files?", "Keep secrets out of commands by referencing environment variables or a secrets manager, ignore agent configuration paths in every repository, enable push protection and secret scanning that covers those paths, and use short-lived credentials so any leak expires quickly."]], "related": ["/ai-architect/", "/fractional-cto/", "/regions/"], "citations": [{"title": "Claude Code's \"Don't Ask Again\" File Exposes Passwords and Keys in 1,122 Public Copies on GitHub", "url": "https://thehackernews.com/2026/11/claude-codes-dont-ask-again-file.html", "publisher": "The Hacker News", "date": "Nov 2026"}, {"title": "\"Don't ask again\" in Anthropic's Claude Code leaks developer secrets", "url": "https://www.itnews.com.au/news/dont-ask-again-in-anthropics-claude-code-leaks-developer-secrets-630203", "publisher": "iTnews", "date": "Nov 2026"}, {"title": "Claude Code approval files found exposing company secrets on GitHub, says Apiiro researchers", "url": "https://itwire.com/business-it-news/security/claude-code-approval-files-found-exposing-company-secrets-on-github-says-apirro-researchers", "publisher": "iTWire", "date": "Nov 2026"}, {"title": "Claude Code settings", "url": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings", "publisher": "Anthropic", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "About push protection", "url": "https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/concepts/secret-security/about-push-protection", "publisher": "GitHub Docs", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "Kubernetes v1.26: GA Support for Kubelet Credential Providers", "url": "https://kubernetes.io/blog/2022/12/22/kubelet-credential-providers/", "publisher": "Kubernetes blog", "date": "22 Dec 2022"}]}-->
<section class="block">
<h2>What happened</h2>
<p>Apiiro reported that when a developer chooses "Yes, and don't ask again" for a command in Claude Code, the tool can save the command, word for word, as an allow rule in <code>.claude/settings.local.json</code> in the project<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. If the command contained a password or API key, so does the file. Apiiro found 104,185 public copies of the file on GitHub, and real secrets in 1,122 of them<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>
<p>Claude Code's documentation describes the local settings file as personal and not checked in, and says Claude Code configures git to ignore it<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Reporting notes that this protection lives in a machine's git settings rather than the repository's own .gitignore, so it does not travel with the repository<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Apiiro's advice: search repositories and their history for these files, stop tracking them and add them to each repository's .gitignore<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Type</b><span>A developer runs a command with a key inline, via the agent</span></li>
<li><b>Approve</b><span>"Don't ask again" saves the exact command as an allow rule</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Store</b><span>The key now sits in a project file under .claude/</span></li>
<li><b>Miss</b><span>Ignore rules are per machine, so another setup or a forced add tracks it</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Publish</b><span>The file is pushed; on a public repository the key is exposed</span></li>
<li><b>Linger</b><span>Deleting the file later leaves it in git history</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Each step is ordinary developer behaviour. The leak comes from the combination.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The wider lesson: agent files are a new place for secrets</h2>
<p>This is not unique to one tool. Coding agents keep settings, approval rules, memory notes, session transcripts and tool configuration on disk, often inside the project directory. Any of them can capture a credential a developer typed or an agent read. Most organisations' secret controls were designed for source code and CI configuration, and do not yet treat agent artefacts as a category.</p>
<p>The underlying habit is also older than AI: pasting a key into a command because it is quicker than setting up proper access. Agents make that habit more expensive, because they record what they are told to do.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Controls that work, in layers</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Habit</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">No secrets in commands<em>reference variables or a secrets manager</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Repository</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Agent paths ignored<em>in every repository's .gitignore</em></span><span>Shared templates<em>new repositories start safe</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Platform</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Push protection<em>block secrets before they land</em></span><span>History scanning<em>find what already leaked</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Credentials</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Short-lived tokens<em>leaks expire quickly</em></span><span>Fast rotation<em>one step, tested</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Policy</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Managed agent settings<em>organisation-wide rules</em></span><span>Approval reviews<em>prune "always allow" lists</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>No single layer is enough; together they make a leaked agent file boring rather than dangerous.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><strong>Keep secrets out of commands.</strong> Teach developers and agents to reference environment variables or a secrets manager rather than pasting values. Approval rules then contain a variable name, not a key.</p>
<p><strong>Ignore agent paths in the repository.</strong> Add agent configuration directories to every repository's .gitignore and to the templates new repositories start from, rather than relying on each developer's machine.</p>
<p><strong>Block at push time and scan history.</strong> GitHub's push protection blocks recognised secrets before they are pushed, and organisations can choose which patterns it covers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Pair it with scanning of existing repositories and their history, including agent directories.</p>
<p><strong>Make leaked credentials short-lived.</strong> The best defence against any leak is a credential that expires before it is useful. Fetch credentials when needed instead of storing them, the pattern Kubernetes uses for registry access<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/south-africa-fintech-machine-agent-identity/short-lived-credentials.webp" alt="Diagram of the kubelet calling a credential provider plugin with a CredentialProviderRequest for an image, the plugin authenticating to the registry and returning a CredentialProviderResponse with credentials" width="1218" height="591" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Fetch credentials on demand rather than writing them down: the same principle keeps agents from collecting secrets. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/blog/2022/12/22/kubelet-credential-providers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes blog, Andrew Sy Kim and Dixita Narang</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>Govern agent settings centrally.</strong> Where your coding agents support organisation-managed settings, use them to set baseline rules, and periodically review the "always allow" lists developers accumulate. See <a href="/insights/ai-coding-policy-engineering-teams/">AI coding policy for engineering teams</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Every new tool on a developer's machine is a new place for secrets to hide. Assume agents will write down what they see, and make sure what they see is never a long-lived key.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What to do this week</h2>
<ul>
<li>Search your organisation's repositories and their history for <code>.claude/</code> and other agent configuration files; rotate any secrets found.</li>
<li>Add agent paths to repository .gitignore files and templates.</li>
<li>Turn on push protection and extend secret scanning to agent directories.</li>
<li>Tell developers, plainly, not to paste credentials into agent prompts or commands.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>The Claude Code finding will be fixed and forgotten, but the pattern will recur with other tools. Coding agents are now part of the software supply chain on every developer's machine, and they generate files, memories and logs that sit outside most security reviews. Treat them like any other privileged tool: managed settings, scanning that covers their files and access through short-lived credentials. Read alongside <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-11-09/">the 9 November tech news</a>, <a href="/insights/agentic-infrastructure-skills-tools-sandboxes/">agentic infrastructure</a> and the <a href="/case-studies/uae-software-house-ai-developer-toolchain-security/">AI developer toolchain security case study</a>.</p>
</section>

